@conciliators_attend
title: Attend Cainholde alongside the Collectivists.
view-if: collectivist_leader == "Conciliators"
on-arrival: left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2; soviet_relation += 1; east_relation += 1

For the first time since the First Conference, the two delegations travel together rather than as rivals with the same destination. The resolutions that come out of Cainholde are unremarkable, as they usually are, but the photograph is not: our people and theirs on the same platform, and no one walking out.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line, the social-fascism language and the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy. In the factory districts and the labour exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted, and our support among the Commons and the unemployed recovers. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so. Its strength grows, and its dissent and the centrists' dissent both fall. Relations with the Collectivists improve considerably.

Abroad, the reaction is warmer still. The <span style="color: #750e0e;">**Central Union**</span> has spent two years insisting that its parties abroad were not isolating themselves, and Cainholde lets it prove the point on its own soil. The <span style="color: #D70926;">Nievalian</span> Reformists, who watched the northern kingdoms' own collectivists follow the hard line into irrelevance, are frankly relieved. Relations with both improve.

@pay
title: Send an approach through the unions.
subtitle: -1 resource. The industrial federations still talk to each other even when the parties do not.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 4)
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Not enough resources (1 needed).
on-arrival: resources -= 1; left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2

We do not approach the party. We approach the men in the industrial federations who share a shop floor with our members whatever the party press says, and we let them make the case upward. A delegation costs nothing, refusing looks like fear, and the unemployed do not care who was right at the split.

It works, barely. The delegation that goes to Cainholde is small and its instructions are stiff, but it goes, and it does not walk out.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line, the social-fascism language and the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy. In the factory districts and the labour exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted, and our support among the Commons and the unemployed recovers. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so. Its strength grows, and its dissent and the centrists' dissent both fall. Relations with the Collectivists improve.

@leftists
title: Let our own Left carry the message.
subtitle: They have standing with the <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> that the rest of us lack, if they are strong enough to use it.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 4)
choose-if: left_strength >= center_strength and left_strength >= labor_strength and left_strength >= reformist_strength and left_strength >= knight_strength
unavailable-subtitle: Our <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> would have to be the strongest faction in the party for Halsmark to take the approach seriously.
on-arrival: left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2

Our Left goes to them as what it is: the largest faction of the largest workers' party on the continent, which Halsmark's people cannot dismiss as easily as they dismiss us. They do not argue that the Collectivists should trust the Marchers. They argue that a party which will not sit in a room with eleven million voters' representatives has chosen to be a sect, and that the men making that choice should say so plainly.

The delegation goes to Cainholde. It is not friendly, and it does not walk out.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line, the social-fascism language and the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy. In the factory districts and the labour exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted, and our support among the Commons and the unemployed recovers. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so. Its strength grows, and its dissent and the centrists' dissent both fall. Relations with the Collectivists improve.

@nothing
title: Spend nothing on Cainholde.
subtitle: Let them decide for themselves.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 4)

We let the question answer itself, and it answers as it has since the Second Conference. Two observers are sent, without instructions to speak. They file a report describing the proceedings as a rehearsal for the next betrayal, and it is printed in full.

@blind
title: The <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> remain blind to the real world.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and not (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 4)

There is nothing to answer. A party that has decided the crisis proves it was right about everything will read Cainholde as one more proof, and no argument of ours could do other than confirm the thesis by being ours.

We go without them, as we did last time.
